{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observed defect\nThree open comment-sweep leaves — improbable-crimson-hare, nonfat-pernicious-aracari, and orthogonal-shrewd-taruca — carry saved PASS plan-review SSHSIG envelopes signed by the original nava-opcert-prod key. Their claims fail `signature: mismatch; ssh-keygen -Y verify rejected signature (exit 255)`. All 3 remain open and unclaimed.\n\nThe envelopes embed the original public key `ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIB6u+ddQiB4ck/ToORQkQwtehODF4VqLirMIUNFpobZZ`, which is still an unrevoked pin in `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`. The same pin file also carries the rotated key `ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIGA6u+J56OuLaR8xsCqyIFZisj/Pke3dkNIfgifykhfi`; a recent certified ticket uses it. An isolated SSHSIG verification of improbable-crimson-hare's unchanged envelope returns `certified` under the historical pin and `mismatch` / exit 255 under the rotated pin.\n\n`src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py::_opcert_trust_root` returns only `_opcert_own_public_keys(tracker)` when the certificate principal equals the current principal. That skips the historical pinned keyring for the same principal. The verifier therefore rejects a valid pre-rotation certificate.\n\nAdjacent finding: MCP `plan_review_status` also returns an output-validation error for these tickets because its declared verdict enum omits `mismatch`; the underlying claim error still exposes the mismatch.\n\n## Decision (operator, 2026-10-24)\nOption 1: for the certificate's own principal, trust this environment's current key plus every pinned key for that principal in `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml` that is not revoked, with no era check. The operator chose this over threading the certificate's tickets-log position into `verify_opcert_record` so key eras apply.\n\n## Verification (2026-10-24, current main)\n- `_opcert_trust_root` (`src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py`) returns only `_opcert_own_public_keys(tracker)` when the principal equals the own principal and an own key exists; it never reads the pinned keyring on that path.\n- `_pinned_environment_keys` returns every keyring entry with a `public_key`, including entries with a non-null `revoked_at_log_position`.\n- The signature verdict reaches `plan_review_status` unchanged (`claim_gate_check` returns `result.get(\"verdict\")`). The verdicts the signature path can produce are `mismatch`, `unknown_principal`, `invalid`, `foreign_key`, `key_not_valid_at_era`, `unknown_scheme`, `unknown_kind`, `unavailable`, `subject_mismatch` and `malformed_statement`, besides `certified` and `unsigned`. `src/rebar/schemas/plan_review_status.schema.json` declares an enum without any of them, so a non-certified signature verdict fails output validation. This confirms the ticket's adjacent finding, and widens it from `mismatch` to the whole set.\n- Not reproduced end to end here: that needs an envelope signed under the pre-rotation key.\n\n## Approach\n1. In `_opcert_trust_root`, when the principal is the own principal: keys = own keys, then pinned keys for that principal whose `revoked_at_log_position` is null, de-duplicated by key body. `trust_basis` stays `own_key` when no extra pinned key was added and becomes `own_key+pinned_environment` when one was, so the label says what the trust root was.\n2. Give `_pinned_environment_keys` an `unrevoked_only` parameter used by the own-principal path only. The foreign-principal path keeps today's behaviour; that path trusting revoked keys is a separate defect, filed as its own bug.\n3. Add the 10 signature verdicts above to the `verdict` enum of `src/rebar/schemas/plan_review_status.schema.json`, and update the verdict lists in the `plan_review_status` docstrings (`src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py`, `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest_gate.py`).\n\n## Scope\nIn scope: `src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py`, `src/rebar/schemas/plan_review_status.schema.json`, the two docstrings, and tests. Out of scope: era validity of pinned keys, the foreign-principal revocation behaviour, and re-signing the 3 comment-sweep leaves.\n\n## Testing\n```\npytest tests/unit/test_opcert_local_verify_binding.py -q\nmake verify\n```\nNew cases, built with two real keys under one principal and a pin file listing both: a certificate signed by the old pinned key verifies after the own key changes to the new one, with `trust_basis` `own_key+pinned_environment`; a certificate signed by a key that is not pinned still returns `mismatch`; a certificate signed by a pinned key marked revoked returns `mismatch`; a certificate with a changed payload still fails; a certificate under the current own key still verifies with `trust_basis` `own_key`. A schema test validates a `plan_review_status` result carrying each of the 10 verdicts. Failure case: the old-key case fails on main with `mismatch`, and the schema case fails on main for `mismatch`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A certificate signed by a pinned, unrevoked historical key of the own principal verifies after the own key has rotated (checked: new test, which fails on main).\n- [ ] A certificate signed by an unpinned key, by a pinned key marked revoked, or with a changed payload or signature still fails (checked: new tests).\n- [ ] `plan_review_status` output validates against its schema for each signature verdict, so a mismatch is returned as a structured verdict (checked: new schema test, which fails on main for `mismatch`).\n- [ ] The era-validity limit of this fix is documented where the own-principal trust root is chosen (checked: comment in the diff).\n- [ ] `make verify` is green on the Linux CI gate (`Verified +1` on the Gerrit change).\n\n## Follow-up\nThe foreign-principal path trusting revoked pinned keys, and era validity for pinned keys, are tracked in their own bug.\n"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1792886243589503001,"uuid":"45cc968c-477a-4818-9e91-6e68b4ae42c7"}